<BaseLayout
  title="Building Your Own MCP Server: Implementation Steps — AI Agent Puzzle"
  description="Turn the previous page's design into runnable code: create the project folder in PowerShell, install mcp, write a minimal server.py, register it with Gemini CLI, swap in the full toolset, and run a complete reflection-draft flow — plus three things that went wrong in testing and the safety boundaries that caught them."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container ai-agent-impl">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">Building Your Own MCP Server: Implementation Steps</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          On the previous page we sketched out the tool surface: four tools, one resource, one prompt. This page <strong>builds it</strong>. Each step ships with full commands. If a step doesn't pass, stop and fix it before moving on.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screens on this page were verified against Gemini CLI v0.55.1, Python 3.13.15, and mcp SDK 1.29.0 on Windows PowerShell.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The main track on this page is the <strong>classroom-machine route</strong>: PowerShell, no VS Code, no administrator rights.<br/>
          If you're on your own laptop and prefer to manage the project with uv, every step has a collapsible alternative.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Part 1: Get a minimal version running ============ -->
      <div class="part-divider">
        <span class="part-label">PART 1</span>
        <span class="part-text">Get a Minimal Version Running</span>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 1 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 1</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Create a Project Folder in PowerShell</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Open a <strong>fresh</strong> PowerShell window, step out of any system folder, and confirm the Python environment:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">cd $HOME</code></pre>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">$env:Path = "$env:LocalAppData\Programs\Python\Python313;$env:LocalAppData\Programs\Python\Python313\Scripts;" + $env:Path</code></pre>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">where.exe python
python --version
python -m pip --version</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Check three things: the first line from <code>where.exe</code> is the <code>…\Programs\Python\Python313</code> one, Python reports 3.13.x, and pip reports a version.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          That second PATH command only affects the current PowerShell window. Close the window and reopen it, and you have to paste it again.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Now create the project folder:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path "$HOME\mcp-test" -Force
cd "$HOME\mcp-test"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If you already created <code>mcp-test</code>, just run the second line, <code>cd</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Create two subfolders:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path "class_notes" -Force
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path "drafts" -Force</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If you already have <code>class_notes</code> and <code>drafts</code>, skip these two lines — <code>dir</code> will confirm.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Finally, drop in a class note so the tools have something to read later. <strong>Everything from <code>@'</code> to <code>'@</code> is one unit: copy the whole block, and the closing <code>'@</code> must sit at the start of its line</strong>:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">@'
MCP is the bridge between a model and its tools.
'@ | Set-Content class_notes\week1.md -Encoding UTF8</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If <code>class_notes</code> already has <code>week1.md</code>, skip this block. Always add <code>-Encoding UTF8</code> for files containing non-ASCII text.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">The resulting layout:</p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--tree" is:raw><code>mcp-test/
├─ server.py          (created in the next step)
├─ class_notes/
│  └─ week1.md
└─ drafts/</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The point: <strong>scope what the AI can touch to this folder.</strong> Don't let it read your whole machine on day one, and don't point it at anything important right away.
        </p>
      </div>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">Own laptop: if you'd rather use VS Code</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click to expand</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            You can open <code>mcp-test</code> with VS Code's <strong>File → Open Folder</strong> and run every command above in the integrated terminal — the effect is identical. The classroom machines don't have VS Code, so this page's main track uses PowerShell throughout.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- STEP 2 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 2</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Install the MCP Package</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Install the MCP Python SDK. This course pins the 1.x line:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">python -m pip install "mcp[cli]>=1.0,<2.0"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Already installed? Skip it. Run <code>python -m pip show mcp</code> first — if you see <code>Version: 1.29.0</code>, there's nothing to do.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Confirm the install:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">python -m pip show mcp</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Verified output (excerpt):</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">Name: mcp
Version: 1.29.0
Summary: Model Context Protocol SDK
Location: C:\Users\your-account\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python313\Lib\site-packages</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Writing <code>python -m pip</code> rather than bare <code>pip</code> means: <strong>use the current Python</strong> to run pip. Machines often have more than one Python, and bare <code>pip</code> may install into an environment you didn't expect.
      </p>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">Own laptop, alternative: manage the project with uv</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click to expand</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            uv gives each project its own isolated environment, which suits your own laptop. If you go the uv route, the <code>command</code> and <code>args</code> values in STEP 5 will differ from what's shown there. This page doesn't cover that — see uv's official documentation.
          </p>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            Make sure you're inside the <code>mcp-test</code> folder, then run:
          </p>
          <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">powershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c "irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex"
uv init</code></pre>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            The first command is uv's official Windows installer. The second <strong>initializes the current folder as a uv project</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">Then run:</p>
          <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">uv add "mcp[cli]>=1.0,<2.0"</code></pre>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            This adds the MCP packages to the project.
          </p>
          <div class="callout-card">
            <p class="callout-text">
              Two reasons to use uv: (1) it keeps the project's environment together, so when Gemini CLI starts the MCP server it can do so via <code>uv run</code>; (2) it makes <strong>package and virtual-env management cleaner and more consistent</strong>, so different projects don't clobber each other.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- STEP 3 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 3</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Write a Minimal MCP Server First</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Run this in PowerShell (copy the whole block; the closing <code>'@</code> must sit at the start of its line):
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">@'
from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP

mcp = FastMCP("Course Notes Assistant")

@mcp.tool()
def hello() -> str:
    """Confirm the class-notes MCP server is up."""
    return "Class-notes MCP server is running."

if __name__ == "__main__":
    mcp.run()
'@ | Set-Content server.py -Encoding UTF8</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This program has <strong>one tool</strong>, <code>hello</code>. It doesn't read files. It doesn't create drafts. It just returns one line.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Small, but important. We need to confirm three things first:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Python runs</li>
        <li>The MCP server starts</li>
        <li>Gemini CLI can find this server</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Once this passes, <strong>adding features afterward is much less likely to send you down a rabbit hole.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 3.5 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 3.5</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Run It Once Directly: Learn to Read "Hanging"</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Before wiring it to Gemini CLI, run it ourselves to confirm the program is alive:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">python server.py</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A warning prints first, and then it <strong>sits there doing nothing</strong>:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">C:\Users\your-account\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python313\Lib\site-packages\pydantic_settings\sources\utils.py:47: IncompleteFieldDefinitionWarning: Field 'lifespan' has an incomplete definition: its annotation contains an unresolved forward reference, so settings sources may fail to correctly resolve its value. Call `model_rebuild()` on the model where the field is defined, once all the referenced types are defined.
  warnings.warn(</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          That warning comes from inside the mcp SDK. It has <strong>nothing to do with your code, and it doesn't affect execution</strong>.<br/>
          The screen going quiet after the warning <strong>is the success case</strong> — the server is standing by, waiting for someone to talk to it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Press <code>Ctrl+C</code> to exit.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        When Gemini CLI starts it later, this warning won't appear on your screen, because the server is launched in the background.
      </p>

      <!-- STEP 4 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 4</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Prepare the API Key</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Create a <code>.gemini</code> folder in the project to hold Gemini CLI's config:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path ".gemini" -Force</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>Replace "paste-your-key-here" on the first line with your own AI Studio key, then copy the whole block</strong> (<code>.env</code> holds ASCII only, so no <code>-Encoding UTF8</code> needed):
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">@'
GEMINI_API_KEY=paste-your-key-here
'@ | Set-Content .gemini\.env</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Confirm it was written:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">Get-Content .gemini\.env</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <code>.env</code> holds your private key. Don't upload it, and don't paste it into a group chat.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 5 -->
      <div class="case-header">
        <p class="case-label">STEP 5</p>
        <h2 class="case-title">Register the MCP Server</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Don't hand-write the config file. Generate it with a Gemini CLI command, then open it and see what it wrote — that way you can't fat-finger a bracket.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>Make sure you're inside the <code>mcp-test</code> folder</strong>, then run:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">npx @google/gemini-cli mcp add course-notes python server.py</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Success prints one line:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">MCP server "course-notes" added to project settings. (stdio)</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          A few blue <code>npm notice</code> lines may follow, telling you a newer npm is available.<br/>
          <strong>That's npx printing its own message. It has nothing to do with us — ignore it, and don't run the update.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That <code>(stdio)</code> tells us the CLI recognized this as a <strong>local stdio server</strong> — it will start a Python program on your machine and talk to it over standard input and output, rather than reaching out to a URL.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Open the generated file and see what it wrote:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">notepad .gemini\settings.json</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">The verified output:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-json">{
  "mcpServers": {
    "course-notes": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": [
        "server.py"
      ]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Only two fields — far simpler than you'd expect:</p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><code>command</code> is "what program to run" — here, <code>python</code></li>
        <li><code>args</code> is "what to feed it" — here, <code>server.py</code></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Together that's <code>python server.py</code> — exactly the line you ran by hand in STEP 3.5. The only difference: this time Gemini CLI runs it for you.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <code>args</code> says <code>server.py</code>, which is a <strong>relative path</strong> — no full folder location.<br/>
          Which means: <strong>you must start Gemini CLI from inside the <code>mcp-test</code> folder</strong>, or it won't find <code>server.py</code>.<br/>
          From now on, the first thing you do in every class is <code>cd "$HOME\mcp-test"</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Now add the allowed-tools list. There's only one tool, <code>hello</code>, so that's all we expose. <strong>Copy the whole block to overwrite the file</strong> (ASCII only, so no <code>-Encoding UTF8</code>):
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">@'
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "course-notes": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": [
        "server.py"
      ],
      "includeTools": [
        "hello"
      ]
    }
  }
}
'@ | Set-Content .gemini\settings.json</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Confirm:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-powershell">Get-Content .gemini\settings.json</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>includeTools</code> means: <strong>of the tools in this server, only the ones on the list can be used.</strong> With a single tool it looks redundant, but this is a habit worth forming on day one.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The <code>mcp add</code> command <strong>overwrites</strong> any existing <code>includeTools</code> setting, and it won't warn you.<br/>
          So the order is always: <strong>run <code>mcp add</code> first, then add <code>includeTools</code></strong> — never the other way round.
        </p>
      </div>

