      <!-- Tested: the AI wrote the file anyway -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Tested: The AI Wrote the File Anyway</h2>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In that last line of the previous section, we explicitly asked it not to create the file yet, and to show us the draft first.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Gemini's summary line read "draft a reflection based on the contents of week1.md, <strong>for the user to confirm</strong>" — so it understood.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">But the tool it then called was:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">?  create_reflection_draft (course-notes MCP Server) {"content":"Through the first week of the course, I learned that Model Context Protocol (MCP) is what connects large language models to ex…</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>create_reflection_draft</code> is the tool that <strong>actually writes a file</strong>. Press <code>Allow once</code> in the confirmation box and the file is created:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">✓  Draft created: My_understanding_of_MCP.md</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        And only then does it ask you: "Please confirm whether this content meets your needs."
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>The file was already written before it asked whether you wanted it.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This isn't a bug in the program, and it isn't a problem with MCP. This is what AI agents actually do:<br/>
          <strong>they misjudge, they skip steps, and they act even when you said "not yet."</strong><br/><br/>
          The only thing that can stop it is that confirmation box.<br/>
          The box says <code>create_reflection_draft</code> in plain sight — one glance tells you it's about to write a file.<br/>
          The right choice at that moment is <strong><code>4. No, suggest changes</code></strong>.<br/><br/>
          This is exactly why this course always teaches <code>1. Allow once</code> rather than "allow for this session."<br/>
          The moment you press "allow everything," nobody is watching this step any more.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tested: the overwrite guard held, then the AI went around it -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Tested: The Overwrite Guard Held, Then the AI Went Around It</h2>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Next we said, "Looks good. Please save it as a reflection draft titled 'My understanding of MCP'."
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Gemini called the tool again with the same title, and hit the guard in the code:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">✓  create_reflection_draft (course-notes MCP Server) {"title":"My understanding of MCP", …

Draft already exists: My_understanding_of_MCP.md. I did not overwrite it. Please use a different title.</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>The boundary held.</strong> The original file was not clobbered.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        But the story doesn't end there. Having seen that reply, Gemini <strong>changed the title to "My deeper understanding of MCP" on its own</strong> and called again — and this time it worked:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">✓  Draft created: My_deeper_understanding_of_MCP.md</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        It never asked whether you wanted a different title; it decided. (The confirmation box did still appear — it just showed the new title.)
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The code's boundary blocked <strong>overwriting</strong>, which is what it was built to do, and it did it.<br/>
          But the AI got around it by changing the title — because "rename it and create another one" is a perfectly legal operation.<br/><br/>
          A boundary can block "actions that shouldn't happen." It cannot block "the AI reaching its own conclusion by legal means."<br/>
          That gap still needs a human reading the confirmation box.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tested: a resource has to be mentioned before it gets read -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Tested: Reading the Resource vs. Not Reading It</h2>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>course://reflection-rules</code> holds five rules. Rule 3 requires the draft to contain three sections: what I understood, where I'm still stuck, how I might apply this.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        But that draft above has <strong>none of them</strong> — it's one continuous block of prose. Rule 4, "if the source material is thin, flag it to the user, don't fill in gaps," wasn't followed either: <code>week1.md</code> is a single sentence, yet the AI expanded it into claims like "safely and in a standardised way, broadening the situations a model can be applied to" — content that simply isn't in the source.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>The resource was sitting right there, and it was not read automatically.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So we asked the AI to go check this server's state. It did the following:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">✓  List MCP Resources    List MCP resources
Listed 1 resources.

✓  Read MCP Resource    Read MCP resource "reflection_rules" from server "course-notes"
Successfully read resource "reflection_rules" from server "course-notes"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        (<code>List MCP Resources</code> and <code>Read MCP Resource</code> are Gemini CLI's own built-in tools, not ours. That's how a resource gets read in.)
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">The draft produced after reading it looked like this:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">I have read and followed the reflection rules in course://reflection-rules,
and rebuilt a compliant draft for you (covering "What I understood", "Where I'm still stuck",
and "How I might apply this"):

- Filename: Week_1_class_reflection.md
- Content:
  - What I understood
    Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an important bridge connecting large language models
    to external tools and data sources.
  - Where I'm still stuck
    I still need more hands-on experience with how MCP handles fine-grained permission
    control and state synchronisation in a genuinely complex, multi-server setup.
  - How I might apply this
    When building custom tools or extending a model's capabilities in future, I can use
    MCP to interface with all kinds of external resources in a standardised way.</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Three tidy sections.</p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          So the nature of a resource is: <strong>a reference book on a shelf, not something memorised.</strong><br/>
          The AI will go look it up when it needs to, but it won't look it up automatically every time.<br/><br/>
          Which is exactly why prompts exist — <strong>a prompt's job is to write "consult course://reflection-rules first" explicitly into the flow</strong>, so that "going to the shelf" no longer depends on the AI's mood.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Tested: the prompt registers, but this CLI version has no entry point -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Tested: The Prompt Registers, but This CLI Version Has No Entry Point</h2>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        On the <code>/mcp list</code> screen, <code>draft_reflection</code> is listed plainly under <code>Prompts:</code>. The server really is offering it.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        But in Gemini CLI v0.55.1, we could not find a way to invoke it:
      </p>

      <ul class="cross-list">
        <li>The autocomplete list after typing <code>/</code> contains only built-in CLI commands (<code>clear</code>, <code>compress</code>, <code>directory</code>, <code>docs</code>, <code>rewind</code>…) — no <code>draft_reflection</code></li>
        <li>Typing <code>/course-notes</code> isn't treated as a command; it's sent to Gemini as an ordinary sentence</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This is not a mistake in your code. The prompt is registered correctly and visible in the listing —<br/>
          <strong>this version of Gemini CLI simply doesn't offer a way to call it</strong>.<br/><br/>
          What a prompt is, why you'd want one, and how it differs from a tool: you already understand that.<br/>
          Whether there's a button to press is the tool's problem, not yours.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Why creating new files is safer than modifying old ones -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Why Creating New Files Is Safer Than Modifying Old Ones</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You might wonder: since the AI can already create drafts, why not let it modify the originals directly? Technically, sure — but <strong>not in version one</strong>. Modifying existing files carries more risk.
      </p>

      <ul class="cross-list">
        <li>If the AI gets it wrong, you might <strong>lose the original content</strong></li>
        <li>If the AI picks the wrong file, recovery is <strong>hard</strong></li>
        <li>If the student isn't familiar with the flow yet, it's <strong>harder to tell where things broke</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So here we let the AI create new drafts only — never modify the originals.
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          The first time you wire up writes,<br/>
          <strong>create new things — don't overwrite old ones.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That's why we keep originals in <code>class_notes/</code> and outputs in <code>drafts/</code>. <strong>Two folders, one workflow — much easier to follow.</strong>
      </p>

      <!-- Safety boundaries in the code -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Safety Boundaries in the Code</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The program is short, but it already has a few boundaries.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Boundary 1: Block weird paths</h3>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-python">name = Path(filename).name</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Stops users from passing things like <code>../secret.txt</code>. We don't want the AI reading anything outside the designated folder.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Boundary 2: .md only</h3>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-python">if path.suffix != ".md":
    raise ValueError("Only .md notes are allowed for now.")</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Restricts reads to Markdown notes.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Try it for real. Type:</p>

      <blockquote class="prompt-example">
        Please read week1.txt.
      </blockquote>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">Reply:</p>

      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-text">✓  read_note (course-notes MCP Server) {"filename":"week1.txt"}

Only .md notes are allowed for now.</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Note the <strong>green check</strong> in front, not a red error. The tool <strong>ran successfully</strong> — it just returned a sentence declining the request.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This is what "speak plainly when something goes wrong" looks like: not a screenful of red, but one readable sentence.<br/>
          Gemini relays it back to you too: "I can't read week1.txt, because that tool currently only allows reading .md note files."
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Boundary 3: Don't overwrite</h3>
      <pre class="code-block" is:raw><code class="lang-python">if path.exists():
    return f"Draft already exists: {filename}. I did not overwrite it. Please use a different title."</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If the draft already exists, <strong>don't overwrite it</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          These look small, but together they make the agent <strong>much less likely to go off the rails</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Common errors -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Common Errors</h2>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">1. Gemini CLI can't find my MCP server</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click for the checklist</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            The most common cause is <strong>starting from the wrong place</strong>. The <code>args</code> in <code>settings.json</code> says <code>server.py</code>, a relative path, so you <strong>must start Gemini CLI from inside the <code>mcp-test</code> folder</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            Run <code>cd "$HOME\mcp-test"</code> before starting; the bottom-left of the status bar should read <code>~\mcp-test</code>. Also confirm <code>.gemini\settings.json</code> really is under <code>mcp-test</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">2. It started, but all the tools are gone</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click for the checklist</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            Check whether you picked <code>1. Trust folder</code> at the trust prompt on startup.
          </p>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            Without trusting the folder, <strong>nothing in <code>.gemini</code> loads</strong> — the MCP server won't appear, and the key in <code>.env</code> won't be read either. Restart and choose to trust it this time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">3. Tools show up but fail when called</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click for the checklist</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            First confirm there are actual <code>.md</code> files inside <code>class_notes</code>. You can also try:
          </p>
          <blockquote class="prompt-example">
            Please use the hello tool to confirm the server is running.
          </blockquote>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            If <code>hello</code> works but <code>list_notes</code> fails, <strong>it's usually a folder or file path issue</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">4. The draft wasn't created</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click for the checklist</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            Check that the <code>drafts</code> folder exists. The program creates it on demand, but if it lacks permission, it can still fail.
          </p>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            Also check that the title doesn't collide with an existing draft. If it does, the program stops and won't overwrite.
          </p>
          <div class="callout-card">
            <p class="callout-text">
              That's <strong>not a bug</strong> — it's a deliberate safety boundary.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="details-card">
        <summary class="details-card__summary">
          <span class="details-card__icon" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <span class="details-card__title">5. The filename on screen doesn't match the real filename</span>
          <span class="details-card__hint">Click for the checklist</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="details-card__body">
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            The CLI renders its messages as Markdown, so an underscore sitting next to a non-word character can be swallowed and turned into italics. With a Chinese title, for instance, the screen shows 「我對<em>MCP</em>的理解.md」 while the real filename is <code>我對_MCP_的理解.md</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="detail-paragraph">
            <strong>The underscores are really in the filename.</strong> Check with <code>dir drafts</code> in PowerShell and you'll see the correct name.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- How this resembles the Claude Code workflow -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">How This Mirrors the Claude Code Workflow</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        We didn't use Claude Code here, but we already practiced a very similar way of working.
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>We <strong>collaborate with the AI in a CLI</strong></li>
        <li>We let the AI <strong>read project data</strong></li>
        <li>We let the AI <strong>call tools based on the task</strong></li>
        <li>We <strong>govern tool boundaries through a config file</strong></li>
        <li>We <strong>break tasks down</strong>, so the AI never does too much at once</li>
        <li>We <strong>preserve human confirmation</strong> instead of letting the AI run free</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So this isn't really about copying any one tool's look. The real lesson is the way of working underneath:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card highlight-card--final">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          Bring the AI into your workspace —<br/>
          <strong>but only with the tools and boundaries it actually needs.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
